clamp — Limit x to a range                                             core · tier 0 · since 0.5.0 · 1.0 · closed form

USAGE
    clamp(x, low, high)

DESCRIPTION
    The value of `x` brought into the range from `low` to `high`: `low` where `x` is below it, `high` where
    `x` is above it, and `x` itself in between. It is `min(max(x, low), high)`, so an Int stays an Int when
    all three are Ints, `NA` in any argument gives `NA`, and when `low` is above `high` the result is
    `high`, as R's `pmin(pmax(x, low), high)` gives. Given a column, it applies to every row in one pass.

ARGUMENTS
    x           Float. The value, or a column of values.
    low         Float. The smallest value the result may have.
    high        Float. The largest value the result may have.

VALUE
    `x` within the range, a Float, or an Int when all three are Ints; a column when `x` is one.

DETAILS
    Exact: the result is one of its three arguments, and nothing is rounded.

ACCURACY
    Closed form.

REFERENCES
    Equivalent to R's `pmin(pmax(x, low), high)`; Stata has no single function (`min(max(x, low), high)`).

SEE ALSO
    min, max, between

EXAMPLES
    > clamp(5, 0, 3)
    3
    > clamp(-1.5, 0, 3)
    0
    > clamp(1.5, 0, 3)
    1.5
    > let d = frame(x = [-2, 0.5, 7])
    > clamp(d["x"], 0, 3)
    Column<Float> · 3 values
    0
    0.5
    3
